  <template v-if="page.path === 'guides/remove-duplicate-rows-csv/'">
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#duplicate-rule">Choose a duplicate rule</a><a href="#spreadsheet-method">Spreadsheet method</a><a href="#datafix-method">DataFix method</a><a href="#validate-output">Validate the result</a></nav>
    <section id="duplicate-rule"><h2>1. Decide what counts as a duplicate</h2><p>Repeated imports, overlapping exports, and copied records can produce duplicate rows. An exact duplicate repeats every field in a row. A key-based duplicate shares selected fields, such as CustomerID, while other values may differ. Those differences may be updates or separate events that you need to retain.</p><p>For example, two orders from the same customer are not duplicate orders. Choose the fields that identify the record itself. Write down the rule before deleting anything, and decide which record to keep when keys match.</p><pre><code>{{ duplicateInput }}</code></pre><p>The third data row repeats the first. Removing whole-row duplicates while keeping the first occurrence leaves three records; the header is not counted as a record.</p><pre><code>{{ duplicateOutput }}</code></pre><p>Names that differ by case or spaces may compare differently across tools. Do not trim or change case just to force matches unless your data rules permit it. Similar-looking records need review.</p></section>
    <section id="spreadsheet-method"><h2>2. Use a spreadsheet to review and remove duplicates</h2><ol><li>Copy the original CSV to a backup, and work on a separate copy. Record the number of data rows and the column headers.</li><li>In Excel, use Data → From Text/CSV to import it. Confirm the delimiter and encoding. Choose Transform Data if you need to set identifier columns to Text before loading. Menu wording varies by Excel version.</li><li>Select the full table, including its header. To inspect likely duplicate keys first, use Home → Conditional Formatting → Highlight Cells Rules → Duplicate Values on the key column. This highlights repeated cell values, not whole-row duplicate groups.</li><li>Choose Data → Remove Duplicates. Confirm that the table has headers. Select all columns for a whole-row comparison, or select only the intended key columns. The operation removes entire rows based on that choice; check the summary before continuing.</li><li>Keep a working XLSX copy if helpful, then save the cleaned sheet to a new CSV filename. CSV stores values, not workbook formulas, formatting, or multiple sheets.</li></ol><p>Spreadsheet comparisons can interpret values differently from a strict text comparison. Test your chosen rule on a small sample, especially for case differences, dates, long numbers, and identifiers. See <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/top-ten-ways-to-clean-your-data">Microsoft’s data-cleaning guidance</a> and <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/keeping-leading-zeros-and-large-numbers">its guidance on preserving identifiers</a>.</p></section>
    <section id="datafix-method"><h2>3. Remove whole-row duplicates with DataFix</h2><p><a :href="pageHref('datafix/')">DataFix</a> implements whole-row duplicate removal that retains the first occurrence. It does not offer selected-key duplicate removal in this workflow.</p><ol><li>Select Open File and open the CSV. Review the detected issues and sample rows.</li><li>Select Remove duplicate rows. Leave other fixes unselected unless you intend those changes too.</li><li>Select Preview Changes. Review the duplicate-removal examples, counts, and any warnings. The preview does not save or modify the file.</li><li>Choose CSV as the export format and select Export Cleaned CSV. Save to a new filename.</li></ol><p>If you need groups based on selected columns, <a :href="pageHref('datafix-magic/')">DataFix Magic</a> provides exact duplicate review using whole rows or chosen keys. Review group differences before selecting removals; a shared key alone does not establish that a record is disposable.</p></section>
    <section id="validate-output"><h2>4. Check the cleaned file before replacing anything</h2><ul><li>Reopen the output with the same delimiter, encoding, and text-column settings.</li><li>Check that output records equal input records minus removed records. In the example: 4 − 1 = 3.</li><li>Confirm that all headers and columns remain, and sample retained and removed groups against the backup.</li><li>Compare important totals, identifiers, accented characters, and leading zeros. Removing rows can change totals; make sure the difference is expected.</li><li>Repeat the duplicate check under the same rule. Keep the backup until the result has been accepted.</li></ul><p>Count parsed records rather than physical text lines: a valid quoted CSV field can contain a newline. For import problems, use the <a :href="pageHref('guides/fix-common-csv-problems/')">CSV troubleshooting guide</a>.</p></section>
  </template>
  <template v-else-if="page.path === 'guides/clean-messy-excel-data/'">
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#protect-workbook">Protect the workbook</a><a href="#clean-values">Clean values step by step</a><a href="#excel-datafix">DataFix workflow</a><a href="#excel-checks">Check the output</a></nav>
    <section id="protect-workbook"><h2>1. Keep the original and define the target</h2><p>Cleaning means making readable data consistent enough for a task. It is different from repairing a workbook that cannot be opened. This tutorial does not cover recovery of a physically corrupted Excel file.</p><p>Save a dated working copy. Identify the intended worksheet and header row, record the number of records, and note formulas and totals that depend on those records. Decide which columns are text identifiers, numbers, dates, or categories before making changes.</p><p>A postal code such as 00123 is an identifier, not a number to calculate with. Preserve it as text at entry or import. Changing the cell format after zeros were lost does not recover the original value; return to the source. See <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/keeping-leading-zeros-and-large-numbers">Microsoft’s leading-zero guidance</a>.</p></section>
    <section id="clean-values"><h2>2. Clean one kind of problem at a time</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Messy and cleaned values"><table><caption>Example cleanup decisions — quotation marks reveal surrounding spaces</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Original value</th><th scope="col">Reviewed value</th><th scope="col">Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>"  Rina  "</td><td>Rina</td><td>Remove surrounding spaces</td></tr><tr><td>delhi / Delhi</td><td>Delhi</td><td>Use an approved category spelling</td></tr><tr><td>00123</td><td>00123</td><td>Keep a text identifier unchanged</td></tr><tr><td>Blank phone</td><td>Blank phone</td><td>Flag for review; do not invent a number</td></tr><tr><td>03/04/2026</td><td>Needs confirmation</td><td>Ask whether it means March 4 or April 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Extra spaces</h3><p>Use a helper column with <code>=TRIM(A2)</code>, fill it down, and compare results before copying and pasting values back. Excel TRIM also collapses repeated ordinary internal spaces; that may change meaningful text. Nonbreaking spaces may require a deliberate substitution, such as <code>=TRIM(SUBSTITUTE(A2,CHAR(160)," "))</code>. Test on a sample and retain the original column.</p><h3>Duplicate records and blank rows</h3><p>Choose whole-row or key-based duplicate rules before using Data → Remove Duplicates. Filter and inspect blank records before deleting entire rows. A blank cell in one column does not mean its row is empty, and a missing amount is not automatically zero. The <a :href="pageHref('guides/remove-duplicate-rows-csv/')">duplicate guide</a> explains how to choose a retention rule.</p><h3>Inconsistent text and missing values</h3><p>List the allowed category values, then use reviewed replacements for misspellings or case differences. Avoid replacing substrings throughout a workbook when only one column should change. Flag missing values for the data owner; fill them only when the replacement has a documented meaning.</p><h3>Mixed types, dates, and formatting</h3><p>Check whether numeric-looking cells are numbers or text and whether separators follow the intended locale. Convert only measurement columns that should be numeric. Confirm date meanings before choosing a consistent display format. Changing number formatting alone does not change the underlying value or turn text into a valid date. Inspect formulas separately from their displayed results.</p><p><a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/top-ten-ways-to-clean-your-data">Microsoft’s cleaning reference</a> describes helper-column and text-cleaning methods. The examples above apply them to explicit decisions, rather than treating every unusual value as an error.</p></section>
    <section id="excel-datafix"><h2>3. Use DataFix for a dataset cleanup export</h2><ol><li>In <a :href="pageHref('datafix/')">DataFix</a>, select Open File and choose the XLSX. Select the intended worksheet.</li><li>Review the analysis. Select only needed options such as Trim whitespace, Remove duplicate rows, or Remove blank rows.</li><li>Select Preview Changes and compare before-and-after examples. Check warnings before exporting.</li><li>Choose Excel Workbook (XLSX), then Export Cleaned XLSX. Save a new file and check it against the original worksheet.</li></ol><p>This is a data-value workflow. It reads saved formula results and exports data, so it should not be used to preserve formulas, styling, charts, or an entire multi-sheet workbook. If those matter, clean a working copy in spreadsheet software instead.</p><p>For recurring checks and compatible recipes, explore <a :href="pageHref('datafix-magic/')">DataFix Magic</a>. It imports the first worksheet only and exports a single sheet; read its import notes and prepare a suitable copy of the intended data.</p></section>
    <section id="excel-checks"><h2>4. Validate the result</h2><p>Compare row and column counts, approved category values, missing-value counts, and representative identifiers. Recalculate relevant totals in your working spreadsheet and explain any changes. Check that dates have the intended meaning and that leading zeros survive reopening. Retain the original workbook and a short record of the cleanup decisions.</p></section>
  </template>
  <template v-else>
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#diagnose-csv">Diagnose the problem</a><a href="#delimiter-quotes">Delimiters and quoting</a><a href="#encoding-types">Encoding and value interpretation</a><a href="#csv-quality">Data quality and validation</a></nav>
    <section id="diagnose-csv"><h2>1. Separate syntax, import settings, and data quality</h2><p>A malformed CSV has structural problems, such as an unclosed quoted field. A valid CSV may open incorrectly because a spreadsheet selected the wrong separator, encoding, or column types. A well-parsed file may still contain duplicate records, extra spaces, or missing values. Each problem needs a different fix.</p><p>Make a backup. Inspect a small sample in a text editor without saving over the original, and compare it with the imported table. If possible, ask the exporting system which delimiter, character encoding, headers, and date conventions it uses. Do not start deleting characters until you understand their purpose.</p></section>
    <section id="delimiter-quotes"><h2>2. Check delimiters, quotes, and column counts</h2><h3>Everything appears in one column</h3><p>A semicolon-separated file opened as comma-separated data may become one column. Use an import dialog to select the correct separator and inspect its preview. For example, <code>CustomerID;Name;City</code> needs a semicolon separator. Do not globally replace commas or semicolons: those characters may be part of real field values. <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/get-started/import-or-export-text-txt-or-csv-files">Microsoft’s text/CSV import instructions</a> explain how to choose import settings.</p><h3>Commas inside a field create extra columns</h3><pre><code>{{ quotedCsv }}</code></pre><p>The quoted address is one field even though it contains a comma. The doubled quotation marks represent a literal quotation mark inside a quoted value. Fields containing separators, quotes, or line breaks need correct quoting; a parser must understand this structure. The <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180.html">CSV conventions in RFC 4180</a> describe this behavior.</p><h3>Rows have inconsistent widths</h3><p>Compare parsed field counts with the header. An unquoted comma can split a value into two fields; a short row may have omitted fields. A trailing comma represents an extra empty field in common CSV parsing. A fully quoted multiline field is not a new record just because the text editor shows a new line.</p><p>Prefer a corrected export from the source system. If you must edit a small file manually, repair only records whose intended field boundaries you can establish. Do not fill or drop fields by guessing, and validate the whole file afterward.</p></section>
    <section id="encoding-types"><h2>3. Preserve characters, dates, and identifiers</h2><h3>Accented characters look wrong</h3><p>Text encoding describes how bytes represent characters. Import with the encoding used by the exporter, and compare known names. UTF-8 can be valid with or without a byte-order mark (BOM); the optional BOM can help some applications recognize UTF-8. Adding a BOM does not convert another encoding into UTF-8. Use an editor’s actual encoding conversion or re-export from the source, keeping the original bytes.</p><p>If characters are already replacement symbols in a saved file, changing the import setting may not recover them. Return to the original export rather than repeatedly saving the damaged interpretation.</p><h3>Dates change or zeros disappear</h3><p>CSV has no cell type declarations. A spreadsheet may turn 00101 into 101 or interpret an ambiguous date according to regional settings. Import IDs, long account numbers, and ambiguous date strings as Text before automatic conversion. Establish the intended date convention separately; an ISO-style year-month-day format helps communicate an agreed date but cannot resolve an unknown one.</p><p>Setting a text format afterward cannot restore digits already discarded. Reimport from the backup with the right column types. See <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/excel/keeping-leading-zeros-and-large-numbers">Microsoft’s identifier preservation reference</a>.</p></section>
    <section id="csv-quality"><h2>4. Clean values only after the file parses correctly</h2><p>Inspect surrounding spaces, empty records, duplicates, and missing values as separate issues. Spaces may be meaningful in a CSV value. An empty field is not the same thing as a row that should be deleted. Choose whether duplicates mean every field matches or only a record key matches, and retain a record of removed rows.</p><p><a :href="pageHref('datafix/')">DataFix</a> can analyze supported data and preview selected cleanup operations before export. <a :href="pageHref('datafix-magic/')">Magic</a> reads CSV values as text, detects comma or semicolon delimiters, and supports validation and duplicate review. Its BOM-aware importer falls back to UTF-8; it has no UI encoding picker. These capabilities do not establish support for every delimiter, legacy encoding, or malformed-file recovery.</p><p>For step-by-step cleanup, continue with the <a :href="pageHref('guides/remove-duplicate-rows-csv/')">duplicate-row guide</a> or <a :href="pageHref('guides/clean-messy-excel-data/')">spreadsheet-cleaning guide</a>.</p><h3>Final validation checklist</h3><ul><li>Reimport the saved file with explicit settings and confirm the column headers and parsed record count.</li><li>Check quoted commas, multiline values, non-ASCII characters, dates, and leading-zero identifiers.</li><li>Compare removed records and important totals with the backup. Confirm that changes follow your cleanup rule.</li><li>Test the output in its destination application before replacing an existing data source.</li></ul></section>
  </template>
